            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Seattle to Maui?</h3>
            <p>Late August through October offers the lowest fares, typically $300 to $550 round-trip. For winter sun escapes, book 60 to 90 days ahead and target January after the holidays. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend pricing year-round.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Seattle to Maui?</h3>
            <p>Alaska Airlines, Delta Air Lines, Hawaiian Airlines, and Southwest all operate daily year-round nonstop flights from Seattle to Kahului. Alaska offers the most frequent flights. Flight duration is approximately five and a half to six hours across the Pacific.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a passport to visit Maui from Seattle?</h3>
            <p>No passport is required for United States citizens traveling to Maui. Hawaii is the 50th state, so standard domestic travel rules apply. A government-issued photo ID or driver's license suffices for TSA screening at Sea-Tac International Airport.</p>

            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Seattle to Maui flight?</h3>
            <p>Southwest allows two free checked bags. Alaska, Delta, and others charge $35 for the first checked bag up to 50 pounds, plus a carry-on and personal item. Surfboards and golf clubs may incur additional fees on Alaska and Delta but fly free on Southwest.</p>

            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Kahului Airport to Wailea or Kaanapali?</h3>
            <p>SpeediShuttle shared rides cost $30 to $60 and reach resorts in 30 to 60 minutes. Rental cars are strongly recommended for Maui exploration. Taxis and Uber run $40 to $90 depending on destination. The Maui Bus connects towns but offers limited resort convenience.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I visit other Hawaiian islands after flying into Maui from Seattle?</h3>
            <p>Yes, inter-island flights connect Kahului to Honolulu, Kona, and Lihue in 25 to 45 minutes. Hawaiian Airlines and Southwest operate frequent service. The Lahaina ferry also connects Maui to Lānaʻi for day trips. No additional TSA screening is required for inter-island flights.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Is Maui safe for tourists from Seattle?</h3>
            <p>Maui is generally very safe, though petty theft occurs at beaches and resort parking lots. Ocean safety requires more attention than crime; strong currents, rip tides, and sharp coral pose real risks. Always heed warning flags and lifeguard instructions at all beaches.</p>

            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Maui for first-time visitors from Seattle?</h3>
            <p>Wailea offers luxury resorts and calm beaches. Kaanapali provides a lively resort strip with Whalers Village. Kihei suits budget travelers with condo rentals and local dining. Hana appeals to adventure seekers. Upcountry Kula works for those prioritizing Haleakalā access and cooler temperatures.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Sea-Tac for my Maui flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive two hours before scheduled departure for domestic flights from SEA. During holiday weekends and winter peak season, consider arriving two and a half hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times at Sea-Tac.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Maui is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Airlines must rebook passengers on the next available flight at no extra cost for weather or operational delays. Unlike international routes, EU compensation rules do not apply. Maintain comprehensive travel insurance for accommodation and activity reimbursement protection.</p>























